 "drivingQuestion": "Why are the renouncers not treated as guests of the one who forgot?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Difficulty answered by the guest-ratio distinction.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "eruvin.69b.16",
   "daf": "69b",
   "sentenceId": 15,
   "he": "נָתְנוּ לוֹ רְשׁוּתָן, הוּא מוּתָּר וְהֵן אֲסוּרִין. וְנֶהֱוֵי אִינְהוּ לְגַבֵּיהּ כִּי אוֹרְחִין! חַד לְגַבֵּי חֲמִשָּׁה — הָוֵי אוֹרֵחַ, חֲמִשָּׁה לְגַבֵּי חַד — לָא הָוֵי אוֹרֵחַ.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «If» the other residents «gave» away «their rights» in the courtyard «to him, he is permitted» to carry from his house into the courtyard, «but they are prohibited» from doing so. The Gemara asks: «But let them,» the ones who renounced their rights in the courtyard, «be» regarded as «guests of his,» which would enable them to carry as well. The Gemara answers: «One vis-à-vis five is» considered «a guest,» whereas «five» or more «vis-à-vis one are not» ordinarily viewed as «guests.»"
  }
 ],
